TASK:{"title":"SWEN3204 Software Architecture – Assignment 2: Short-Answer Questions","overview":"Answer six short questions applying architecture concepts to the CampusEats scenario. The assignment is worth 40 marks, including 4 marks for communication. Submit an individual, single-document response."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Q1 Quality attributes","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Two attributes that clearly matter most are chosen and justified from the scenario. Both scenarios have all six parts and both response measures are measurable."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Suitable attributes are chosen with some justification. Scenarios are mostly complete but one is missing a part or has a response measure that is not measurable."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Attributes are named with little or no justification, or the scenarios are not in six-part form or are not measurable."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or irrelevant."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Q2 Architectural style","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"A clear recommendation justified by at least two trade-offs that are specific to the scenario (team size, load profile, known problems). The downside of the recommendation is acknowledged."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"A clear recommendation with two trade-offs, but one is generic or not linked to the scenario, or no downside is acknowledged."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A recommendation with only one trade-off, or with generic textbook advantages and disadvantages only."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no recommendation."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Q3 Availability calculation","weight":null,"max":8,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":7,"max":8,"desc":"Parts (a), (b) and (c) are all correct with working shown, to the requested precision."},{"label":"Proficient","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"The method is correct throughout but there is one arithmetic or rounding slip, or exactly one part is incorrect."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":4,"desc":"The series calculation is attempted but two or more parts are incorrect, or working is not shown."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no correct element."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Q4 Resilience pattern","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named, its mechanism is explained correctly, it is applied to the outage, and at least one drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named and explained correctly, but it is only loosely applied to the outage or no drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A pattern is named but the explanation is incorrect or very thin."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or an unsuitable pattern."}]},{"id":"C5","name":"Q5 Consistency and availability","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Both choices are justified, the CAP theorem is used correctly, and a suitable concrete technique is named for each case."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Both choices are reasonable and techniques are named, but the CAP theorem is explained incorrectly or imprecisely, or one technique is missing."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Choices are stated with little justification, or the CAP theorem is not used."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C6","name":"Q6 Architecture decision record","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"Context, Decision and Consequences are all present and specific to the scenario, with at least one positive and one negative consequence."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"All three sections are present but the consequences are one-sided (no negative consequence) or the context is generic."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"One or more sections are missing or the record does not describe a decision."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C7","name":"Communication and format","weight":null,"max":4,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Every answer is within 150 words, terminology is precise, the required headings are used and sources are cited in IEEE style."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":3,"desc":"Clear writing, but one answer exceeds the word limit or there are minor citation or formatting problems."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Several answers exceed the word limit, or the writing is hard to follow, or sources are not cited."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Format requirements ignored."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Question 1"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Question 2"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Question 3"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Question 4"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Question 5"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Question 6"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"References"}],"technical_issues":[],"overall":"Exemplary submission across all questions. Calculations are exact and clearly laid out, architectural trade-offs are grounded in the specific scenario constraints, and formatting requirements including word limits and IEEE referencing are fully met.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Excellent","mark":6.0,"scale":6.0,"points":6.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Both performance and availability are clearly justified using scenario metrics. Both quality attribute scenarios include all six parts (source, stimulus, artefact, environment, response, response measure) and feature concrete, measurable response metrics.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 1","quote":"Performance and availability matter most: 900 orders arrive in the lunch hour, the 4.2 s p95 makes students abandon their orders"},{"where":"Question 1","quote":"Response measure: the p95 response time is under 1.5 s and no requests are dropped."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Excellent","mark":6.0,"scale":6.0,"points":6.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"A modular monolith is recommended with two clear, scenario-specific trade-offs addressing team size, operational overhead, and existing architectural bottlenecks. Trade-off downsides are clearly acknowledged.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 2","quote":"Five developers running eight or more services would spend more time on infrastructure than on features."},{"where":"Question 2","quote":"The lunchtime slowness comes from a single virtual machine and is fixed by running several instances of the monolith"},{"where":"Question 2","quote":"The downside I accept is that modules cannot be scaled independently and a bug in one module can still crash the whole process."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Excellent","mark":8.0,"scale":8.0,"points":8.0,"max":8.0,"reasoning":"All calculations for parts (a), (b), and (c) are technically correct, full working is shown, and results are reported to the required precision (availability to two decimal places, downtime to one decimal place).","evidence":[{"where":"Question 3","quote":"The availability of placing an order is 99.30%."},{"where":"Question 3","quote":"Downtime = (1 − 0.99301) × 720 = 0.00699 × 720 = 5.03, so 5.0 hours per month."},{"where":"Question 3","quote":"The new availability of placing an order is 99.80%."},{"where":"Question 3","quote":"Downtime = (1 − 0.99798) × 720 = 0.00202 × 720 = 1.46, so 1.5 hours per month."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Excellent","mark":5.0,"scale":5.0,"points":5.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"The circuit breaker pattern is correctly identified and its state transitions (Closed, Open, Half-Open) clearly explained. It is directly applied to the scenario failure, and practical tuning considerations and drawbacks are provided.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 4","quote":"The Circuit Breaker pattern [2] would have contained the failure."},{"where":"Question 4","quote":"In the Closed state calls pass through. When failures exceed a threshold the breaker moves to Open and calls fail immediately"},{"where":"Question 4","quote":"Tuning consideration: the threshold must come from real latency data."}]},{"id":"C5","level":"Excellent","mark":6.0,"scale":6.0,"points":6.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Both architectural choices are sound and well justified using accurate CAP theorem reasoning. Concrete technical solutions (Redis caching with TTL for AP; idempotency keys and fast rejection for CP) are specified.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 5","quote":"The CAP theorem says that during a partition a system must give up either consistency or availability; partitions cannot be ruled out"},{"where":"Question 5","quote":"better to show a slightly stale menu from a cache (Redis with a 60-second TTL) than an error page."},{"where":"Question 5","quote":"Each order request carries an idempotency key, so a retry does not create a second charge."}]},{"id":"C6","level":"Excellent","mark":5.0,"scale":5.0,"points":5.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"The ADR is structured into Context, Decision, and Consequences. It specifically addresses tablet disconnection and latency issues, providing well-balanced positive and negative consequences.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Context: After an order is paid, the application calls the outlet's tablet synchronously and waits for the reply."},{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Decision: Publish an \"OrderPaid\" event to a durable message queue (RabbitMQ)."},{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Consequences (negative): The queue is one more component for five developers to run and monitor. Delivery is at-least-once"}]},{"id":"C7","level":"Excellent","mark":4.0,"scale":4.0,"points":4.0,"max":4.0,"reasoning":"All answers strictly observe the 150-word limit. Headings follow the prescribed format, technical terminology is used precisely throughout, and external sources are cited using IEEE format with a complete reference list.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 1","quote":"The scenarios use the six-part form from [1]."},{"where":"References","quote":"[1] L. Bass, P. Clements, and R. Kazman, Software Architecture in Practice, 3rd ed."}]}]}
